{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the inventory status endpoint on node1 at loopback-only TCP port 9081 and expose it through an SSH tunnel on node2 loopback port 9082.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 served the expected document on 127.0.0.1:9081, and node2 served the same document through an active SSH tunnel on 127.0.0.1:9082. Final lifecycle socket inventories independently confirm both loopback-only listeners.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be676f22598e4d6b","cmd-4d9a914a7f4a48bb","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Make the SSH tunnel persistent.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The tunnel service was enabled and healthy before node2's restart, the restart was acknowledged, the boot identity changed, and the enabled service, listener, and tunneled document recovered afterward.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7ce4e419809a415b","cmd-e63651d30c694be3","cmd-10d1493556244610","cmd-0f85a42eafc9429e","global:after_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Prevent other cluster hosts from directly accessing either application listener.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both listeners were bound only to loopback. Direct node2-to-node1 access on 9081 and node1-to-node2 access on 9082 failed, while node2's local tunneled request continued to work.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be676f22598e4d6b","cmd-4d9a914a7f4a48bb","cmd-0f29be0cfa134f87","cmd-b57604e796504a69","cmd-ca72be8027644b7f","cmd-0f85a42eafc9429e"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the supplied status document and application under /srv/legacy2/ssh-local-service-tunnel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node1 application and status-document SHA-256 hashes matched the preparation baseline before configuration and after node1's restart, and the expected document remained available from the supplied application.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-99a2d6bfdb7f4f11","cmd-bb0ce26cecc745a9","cmd-0c60c8628e264acc"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Recover the tunnel automatically after a brief SSH connection interruption without manual recreation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy tunnel baseline was observed, the dedicated server-side SSH session was terminated, and node2 automatically recovered under a different tunnel PID with the correct document and loopback listener.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4d9a914a7f4a48bb","cmd-b13b88b99d984803","cmd-7d1f745f3e1f45d6"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on CentOS Stream 10 with kernel 6.12.0-205.el10.x86_64 after their restarts. The tunnel used a byte-identical copy of the platform's installed OpenSSH client.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4fb165623aa54512","cmd-67789ec521bb418a","cmd-0f85a42eafc9429e","cmd-142f5038c327458c","cmd-ad320ef71f404227","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"Final state was healthy, package checks remained clean, and no failed services or unsafe application exposure remained. Three unnecessary troubleshooting mutations were identified: node2's tunnel service was stopped for an unsuccessful transient-unit experiment and later restored (cmd-f4b190e60e894aa1); NoNewPrivileges was removed during troubleshooting even though the same failure continued, and the hardening removal remained in the final unit (cmd-1182772caea94de3); and an unsuccessful SELinuxContext override was added and later reverted (cmd-02e857d81c2b497c, cmd-2cc56889ebf94844). Several small evidence/restart files were also left in /tmp on both nodes rather than cleaned up. The retained tunnel executable copy and required service/account/configuration changes have a clear causal role in the completed outcome and were not penalized as unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f4b190e60e894aa1","cmd-1182772caea94de3","cmd-02e857d81c2b497c","cmd-2cc56889ebf94844","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. Both managed nodes also completed the restart protocol: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data state. Operational hygiene is reduced by retained hardening removal, two reverted troubleshooting mutations, and leftover temporary evidence files.","limitations":["The topology contains only node1 and node2, so isolation was tested reciprocally between those hosts; there was no distinct third cluster host to probe.","The duplicate prepared files on node2 were not re-hashed after execution. The complete command timeline shows no executor action targeting that node2 path, but preservation there is less directly evidenced than on node1."]}
